w: Him- * self hath free- ly gi- ven To shame and blows and bit- ter death.
w: From * His great heart there flo- weth Sigh af- ter sigh of an- guish o'er.
w: Of * sin and dire trans- gres- sion, Thou deeds of e- vil dost not know.
w: With * which Thy soul is cum- bered, Thy sor- rows raised by wi- cked hands.
w: The * floods a- round Thee sur- ging, 'Tis I who have de- served them well.
w: The _ crown for me se- cu- ring, My heal- ing in Thy wounds is found.
w: My _ place with pa- tience fil- ling, From sin and guilt hast made me free.
w: Thy _ grave its grave Thou ma- kest; Of love, O u- ne- xampled fi- re!
w: All _ that my life can ren- der And all I have to Thee re- sign.
w: Till _ soul from bo- dy se- ver, My heart re- mem- ber shall for aye.
w: Thy _ love and mer- cy sea- ling, The pledge of truth and con- stan- cy.
w: How _ sore- ly He re- qui- teth— All this Thy suf- ferings teach my soul.
w: Whose _ ma- lice aye doth move them To grieve me by their wick- ed- ness.
w: Un- _ to my neigh- bor of- fer For- give- ness of each bit- ter smart.
w: There _ I'll for aye be leav- ing, As much as in my strength doth lay.
w: Be _ guid- ing and at- tend- ing My way to Thine e- ter- nal rest.
